=== layout: subsection

# 5. The Durham-Humphrey Amendment of 1951

=== layout: one-panel

# 5. Questions (1 of 2)

@body

- Why did Congress decide in 1951 to separate prescription drugs from those sold over the counter?
- How did this law change the daily role of pharmacists and physicians in controlling drug access?
- What labeling requirements did it create to help consumers use medications safely?
- How did the amendment reduce the risk of misuse and self-medication with potent new drugs?
- Why is the phrase "Caution: Federal law prohibits dispensing without prescription" important in drug labeling history?
- How did this law lead to later drug scheduling systems under the Controlled Substances Act of 1970?

=== layout: one-panel

# 5. Questions (2 of 2)

@body

- How did the Controlled Substances Act classify drugs into Schedules I-V, and what criteria were used for each class?
- How did the Controlled Substances Act formalize federal control, and when was the Drug Enforcement Administration (DEA) established?
- How have prescription laws and controlled-substance schedules together shaped modern pharmaceutical regulation in the United States?

=== layout: one-panel

# 5. Some Drugs Need Supervision

@body

- The 1951 amendment clarified the distinction between prescription and over-the-counter drugs.
- Some products cannot be used safely without professional supervision because of toxicity, method of use, or other risks.
- Prescription status connects access to a prescriber and pharmacist. It does not mean the product is a controlled substance.

@citation

https://www.fda.gov/about-fda/fda-history/milestones-us-food-and-drug-law

=== layout: one-panel

# 5. Two Separate Classifications

@body

- Prescription versus OTC concerns conditions for safe use and dispensing.
- Controlled-substance schedules concern abuse potential, accepted medical use, and dependence-related controls under the Controlled Substances Act.
- Many prescription drugs are not controlled substances. Antibiotics and insulin illustrate why the categories cannot be used interchangeably.
- The CSA dates to 1970; DEA was established in 1973. The CSA did not itself create DEA in 1970.

@citation

https://www.fda.gov/about-fda/fda-history/milestones-us-food-and-drug-law

=== layout: one-panel

# 5. Compare the Rules on Two Bottles

@body

- An antibiotic may require a prescription without belonging to a CSA schedule.
- An opioid can require both a prescription and additional controlled-substance safeguards.
- For each product, identify the reason for professional supervision, who may dispense it, and any additional storage, recordkeeping, or refill restrictions.
- The important distinction is the purpose of each control, not memorizing two lists.

@citation

https://www.fda.gov/about-fda/fda-history/milestones-us-food-and-drug-law

=== layout: big-image

@image

![5. What Does the Prescription Requirement Do?](assets/set6_sources/topic_05_030.jpg)

@caption

Professional supervision and controlled-substance scheduling serve different purposes. Which risks does requiring a prescription address?

=== layout: subsection

# 6. The Kefauver-Harris Amendments of 1962

=== layout: one-panel

# 6. Questions

@body

- How did the thalidomide disaster influence the 1962 amendments, and what happened differently in the United States?
- How did the Kefauver-Harris law change drug approval from "safe" to "safe and effective"?
- Why did the amendments require informed consent for people in clinical trials?
- How did this act give the FDA the power to control drug advertising and truth in labeling?
- How did the new evidence-based rules improve the quality of research data submitted for drug approval?
- How did these changes influence how modern clinical trials are designed and reviewed today?

=== layout: one-panel

# 6. A Useful Mechanism Is Not Enough

@body

- The thalidomide disaster exposed severe fetal harms. Frances Kelsey resisted U.S. approval because the submitted safety evidence was inadequate.
- The 1962 amendments required substantial evidence that new drugs are effective, as well as evidence supporting safety.
- Drug development became a question of what properly designed investigations show, rather than what a persuasive advertisement promises.

@citation

https://www.fda.gov/about-fda/fda-history/milestones-us-food-and-drug-law

=== layout: one-panel

# 6. Ask What the Study Can Establish

@body

- A useful trial defines the population, treatment, comparison, and outcome before examining the result.
- Controls help distinguish a treatment effect from disease variation, expectations, and other changes.
- Consent, investigator oversight, adverse-event reporting, and reliable records protect participants and the interpretability of the evidence.
- The amendments strengthened clinical-investigation controls and FDA oversight of prescription-drug advertising.

@citation

https://www.fda.gov/about-fda/fda-history/milestones-us-food-and-drug-law

=== layout: one-panel

# 6. Different Evidence, Different Claims

@body

- A receptor binds the drug in a dish: evidence about a molecular interaction.
- Symptoms improve after treatment in one patient: an observation with many possible explanations.
- A well-designed controlled study shows a clinically meaningful benefit with acceptable risks: evidence supporting a treatment claim.
- Keep these steps connected without pretending they are interchangeable.

@citation

https://www.fda.gov/about-fda/fda-history/milestones-us-food-and-drug-law

=== layout: big-image

@image

![6. A New Standard for Drug Evidence](assets/set6_sources/topic_06_032.jpg)

@caption

A historical announcement of stronger drug law makes the shift visible. Safety, effectiveness, consent, and reliable investigations need evidence rather than promises.

=== layout: subsection

# 7. Plant Breeders' Rights and Farmers' Rights

=== layout: one-panel

# 7. Questions (1 of 2)

@body

- What are Farmers' Rights, especially in developing countries?
- How do these rights protect traditional agricultural practices and biodiversity?
- What is the Plant Variety Protection Act (PVPA), and what rights does it grant to plant breeders?
- How long does PVPA protection last compared with patent protection?
- How do Plant Breeders' Rights under the PVPA differ from Farmers' Rights?
- What conflicts arise, such as limits on seed replanting, and how might they be resolved?

=== layout: one-panel

# 7. Questions (2 of 2)

@body

- What is the terminator gene technology, and what implications does it have for sustainable agriculture?
- How could it affect seed-saving traditions, biodiversity, and farmers' livelihoods?
- How do international treaties like the International Treaty on Plant Genetic Resources for Food and Agriculture (ITPGRFA) balance breeders' incentives with farmers' rights?

=== layout: one-panel

# 7. Several Rights Can Apply to One Seed

@body

- U.S. plant variety protection covers eligible new, distinct, uniform, and stable varieties. Protection generally lasts 20 years, or 25 for trees and vines.
- Utility patents, plant patents, contracts, and plant variety certificates are different tools with different coverage and exceptions.
- The right to save or replant seed depends on which rights and agreements apply. Do not infer a universal permission from the word seed.

@citation

https://www.ams.usda.gov/services/plant-variety-protection

=== layout: one-panel

# 7. Seed Saving and Sterility Differ

@body

- Hybrid seeds may produce a variable next generation because alleles segregate; that does not make all hybrid seed sterile.
- Genetic use restriction technology is a proposed biological strategy for restricting propagation or trait use. It is not an explanation for every restriction on commercial seed saving.
- Distinguish biological inheritance, patent or variety rights, and a license agreement before deciding what prevents replanting.

@citation

https://www.ams.usda.gov/services/plant-variety-protection

=== layout: one-panel

# 7. Whose Rights, in Which Country?

@body

- Farmers' rights include recognition of contributions to crop diversity, benefit sharing, and participation in decisions under international frameworks.
- India's 2001 plant-varieties and farmers' rights law is not a U.S. statute.
- The international treaty leaves implementation of farmers' rights to national governments, subject to national law.
- Compare incentives to develop a variety with continued access to seed, breeding resources, and locally adapted diversity.

@citation

https://www.ams.usda.gov/services/plant-variety-protection

=== layout: big-image

@image

![7. Different Reasons a Saved Seed May Disappoint](assets/set6_drawings/seed_saving.drawn.svg)

@caption

Biological segregation, sterility, and restrictions on use are distinct explanations. Identify which one applies.

